<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Korošec</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Korošec was a Slovene Catholic priest and politician who presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> proclaimed in October 1918. He led the Slovene People's Party for two decades and served in many governments of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, becoming its only non-Serb prime minister in 1928 to 1929.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-korosec.jpg" alt="Anton Korošec" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: o.Ang. · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-2010-0420-501,_Anton_Korosec.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anton Korošec was born on 12 May 1872 in Biserjane, then in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, and went to school in Ptuj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maribor">Maribor</a>. He studied theology and was ordained a priest in 1895, then took a doctorate in theology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-graz">University of Graz</a> in 1905. A friend of Janez Evangelist Krek, he adopted Krek's political views. He became known as an orator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1907 Korošec was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsrat">Reichsrat</a> for the Slovenian People's Party and, as president of the Yugoslav Club, read out the May Declaration, which called for all South Slavs to be united in one state unit within the Austro-Hungarian monarchy. After the break-up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the National Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, which he presided over, declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> on 29 October 1918.</p><p>Earlier he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> had agreed the Geneva Declaration, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> recognised the equal rights of the components of such a state. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> that followed was nonetheless a unitary monarchy. He was vice-president in its first government and again from 1924, and minister of the interior in 1924 and 1927. He opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidovdan-constitution">Vidovdan Constitution</a> and campaigned for Slovene autonomy.</p><p>After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> in 1928 the king called Korošec to lead the first Yugoslav government without a Serbian prime minister, but dismissed him when the January 6th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">Dictatorship</a> was proclaimed. He served in Petar Živković's government in 1929, which fell in 1930. In opposition he drew up the Slovenian Declaration, calling for a new multinational union, and was exiled to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Hvar</a> in 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1935 Korošec became minister of the interior under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-stojadinovic">Milan Stojadinović</a> and urged close relations with Nazi Germany. He later served as minister of education in the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dragisa-cvetkovic">Dragiša Cvetković</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a>. In October 1940 he introduced two antisemitic laws, one limiting the participation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the wholesale food industry and the other limiting the number of Jewish students in secondary schools and universities.</p><p>When other ministers objected, he insisted that refusing would endanger relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. As interior minister he declared all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Communists and Freemasons traitors, conspirators and enemies of the state, and he organised the anti-Communist Sentinel in the Tempest. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 14 December 1940, aged 68.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anton Korošec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 29 October the Croatian Sabor broke ties with Hungary and Austria, and the new state was proclaimed, covering Slovenia, Croatia-Slavonia, Dalmatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Vojvodina, with the Slovene politician <b>Anton Korošec</b> as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Biserjane, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.57,16.03&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Biserjane on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4927 526 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anton Korošec"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5377 796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5377 796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.57,16.03&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
